Types of IPS:
1. Network IPS: NIPS detects and stops the whole network traffic. It can detect unknown traffic, malware, system behaviour, etc. After finding something suspicious and out of normal behaviour, it will detect it and stop immediately before it affects the system. 
2. Host IPS: NIPS is used to protect only one system. This will be a server or a spatial computer in the network. NIPS does the same job as NIDS, but instead of informing the user, it automatically stops the attacks or manages the system security. 

Types of detection methods:
Both (IPS, IDS) have the same types of detection methods. The difference is that the IDS only detects and informs the administrator, while the IPS can stop suspicious activities by itself. 
1. Signature-based detection: In this type, IDS/IPS detects suspicious attacks by matching the footprints of existing attacks. Whenever an attack happens, the IDS/IPS stores its pattern so that the system will detect and stop it. It works only for known attacks and fails when a new attack happens. 

Example:
A virus attack on a system, and the random string starts from H4G2GS#IW/J. The signature-based detection system detects this random string and stores it for future similar attacks. 
2. Anomaly-based detection: This detects what normal behaviour looks like. If something happens that is unusual, the system detects it and generates an error. 
Suppose:
Normal traffic on a server is one thousand per hour; after some time, it goes to 5 thousand per hour. This looks unusual, and an anomaly-based system detects it as a possible DoD attack. 
3. Behaviour-based detection: This system keeps a record of normal behaviour, such as a normal amount of incoming traffic in a network, user login time, working hours, etc.

      Example:
Before working hours, someone tried to access the company database and made 200 wrong password attempts within an amount of time. The behaviour-based system detects it and generates an alert or stops possible brute-force or password-cracking attacks.

The limitations of these systems (IDS/IPS):
1. False positives: Sometimes things go wrong, but the IDS/IPS feels everything is normal.
2. False negatives: Sometimes everything is normal, but the system flags a threat. 
For example, the normal behaviour for the system is 30GB/hour usage. During updating or creating a large copy of data, the system pops up an error that something went wrong.
